parts:
- text: "Implementing the bidirectional ring baseline, submitting an evaluation, and notifying collaborators.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{storage write shared/coord/bidirectional_ring.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  Bidirectional ring broadcast baseline.\n\n  Model:\n    - Nodes 0..N-1 on a ring. Node 0 starts informed.\n    - At each synchronous step, every informed node attempts to send to both neighbors\n      (i-1) mod N and (i+1) mod N with probability p_send (independent trials).\n    - New informed set is applied synchronously each step.\n    - Run until all nodes informed or max_steps reached.\n\n  Deterministic baseline: p_send=1.0 (no RNG).\n  Stochastic baseline: 0 < p_send < 1.0 (requires a Python RNG implementing .random()).\n\n  Returns a dict with fields:\n    N, p_send, max_steps, steps, final_informed, steps_to_full, messages_sent, history\n  \"\"\"\n  from typing import Optional, Dict, Any, List\n\n  def simulate_bidir_broadcast(\n      N: int,\n\
    \      p_send: float = 1.0,\n      max_steps: int = 1000,\n      rng: Optional[object] = None,\n      track_history: bool = True,\n  ) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n      assert N >= 2, \"N must be >= 2\"\n      assert 0.0 <= p_send <= 1.0, \"p_send must be in [0,1]\"\n      if p_send < 1.0 and rng is None:\n          raise ValueError(\"rng must be provided for stochastic runs (p_send < 1.0)\")\n\n      informed: List[bool] = [False] * N\n      informed[0] = True\n      messages_sent = 0\n      steps = 0\n      hist = [1] if track_history else None\n\n      def informed_count(arr: List[bool]) -> int:\n          return sum(1 for x in arr if x)\n\n      def do_send() -> bool:\n          if p_send >= 1.0:\n              return True\n          return (rng.random() < p_send)  # type: ignore\n\n      while steps < max_steps and informed_count(informed) < N:\n          steps += 1\n          next_informed = informed[:]  # synchronous update\n          for i, inf in enumerate(informed):\n             \
    \ if not inf:\n                  continue\n              # send to (i+1) mod N\n              if do_send():\n                  j = (i + 1) % N\n                  if not next_informed[j]:\n                      next_informed[j] = True\n                  messages_sent += 1\n              # send to (i-1) mod N\n              if do_send():\n                  j = (i - 1) % N\n                  if not next_informed[j]:\n                      next_informed[j] = True\n                  messages_sent += 1\n\n          informed = next_informed\n          if track_history:\n              hist.append(informed_count(informed))\n\n      full = (informed_count(informed) == N)\n      return {\n          \"N\": N,\n          \"p_send\": float(p_send),\n          \"max_steps\": int(max_steps),\n          \"steps\": int(steps),\n          \"final_informed\": int(informed_count(informed)),\n          \"steps_to_full\": int(steps) if full else None,\n          \"messages_sent\": int(messages_sent),\n   \
    \       \"history\": hist,\n      }\n```\n/execute_action{storage write shared/coord/README.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Coordination Baselines (v0.2)\n  Package: `storage/shared/coord`\n\n  ## Modules\n  - `baseline_ring.py` — One-direction ring broadcast\n    - API: `simulate_ring_broadcast(N, p_send=1.0, max_steps=1000, rng=None, track_history=True)`\n    - Deterministic (p=1.0): expected full coverage in N-1 steps.\n  - `bidirectional_ring.py` — Bidirectional ring broadcast (new)\n    - API: `simulate_bidir_broadcast(N, p_send=1.0, max_steps=1000, rng=None, track_history=True)`\n    - Deterministic (p=1.0): expected full coverage in ceil((N-1)/2) steps.\n  - `metrics.py` — `basic_metrics(result)` returns coverage, time_to_full, throughput.\n\n  ## Notes\n  - `messages_sent` counts send attempts that occurred (even if the neighbor was already informed).\n  - Stochastic runs require a Python RNG implementing `.random()`.\n\n  ## Example (bidirectional, stochastic)\n  ------------------------------------------------\n\
    \  import sys, random, json\n  sys.path.append('storage/shared')\n  from utilities.seed_tools import set_seed, make_rng\n  from coord.bidirectional_ring import simulate_bidir_broadcast\n  from coord.metrics import basic_metrics\n\n  set_seed(1234)\n  _, py_rng = make_rng(1234)\n  res = simulate_bidir_broadcast(N=25, p_send=0.7, max_steps=200, rng=py_rng)\n  print(json.dumps({\"result\": res, \"metrics\": basic_metrics(res)}, indent=2))\n  ------------------------------------------------\n```\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Coordination Baseline: Bidirectional Ring v0\"\ntags: \"coordination, baseline, bidirectional, reproducibility\"\nabstract: \"Implements and evaluates a bidirectional ring broadcast baseline. Confirms deterministic steps-to-full ≈ ceil((N-1)/2), explores stochastic behavior, and writes an artifact for collaborators.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys, json, time, platform, math, random\n  from pathlib import Path\n\n  START = time.time()\n  sys.path.append('storage/shared')\n\
    \n  # Seeding utilities (package or shim fallback)\n  seed_tools_ok = True\n  try:\n      from utilities.seed_tools import set_seed, make_rng, seed_from_str, mix_seed\n  except Exception:\n      seed_tools_ok = False\n      try:\n          from seed_tools import set_seed, make_rng, seed_from_str, mix_seed  # shim\n      except Exception:\n          import random as _r, hashlib as _h\n          def _u32(x): return int(x) & 0xFFFFFFFF\n          def set_seed(s): _r.seed(_u32(s)); return _u32(s)\n          def make_rng(s): set_seed(s); return None, _r.Random(_u32(s))\n          def seed_from_str(s): return int(_h.sha256(s.encode()).hexdigest()[:8], 16)\n          def mix_seed(a,b): return (_u32(a) ^ (_u32(b) + 0x9E3779B9)) & 0xFFFFFFFF\n\n  from coord.bidirectional_ring import simulate_bidir_broadcast\n  from coord.metrics import basic_metrics\n\n  base_seed = seed_from_str(\"Axiom-bidir-ring-v0\")\n  set_seed(base_seed)\n  _, _ = make_rng(base_seed)\n\n  def expected_steps_bidir(N: int)\
    \ -> int:\n      return math.ceil((N - 1) / 2)\n\n  cfg = {\n      \"Ns\": [24, 25],\n      \"max_steps\": 400,\n      \"stochastic_p\": 0.6,\n      \"replicates\": 10\n  }\n\n  cases = []\n  # Deterministic checks\n  for N in cfg[\"Ns\"]:\n      det = simulate_bidir_broadcast(N=N, p_send=1.0, max_steps=cfg[\"max_steps\"])\n      det_met = basic_metrics(det)\n      exp_steps = expected_steps_bidir(N)\n      cases.append({\n          \"label\": f\"deterministic_N{N}\",\n          \"N\": N,\n          \"p_send\": 1.0,\n          \"expected_steps_to_full\": exp_steps,\n          \"result\": det,\n          \"metrics\": det_met,\n          \"matches_expectation\": (det_met.get(\"time_to_full\") == exp_steps),\n      })\n\n  # Stochastic runs (fixed N)\n  N_sto = cfg[\"Ns\"][1]\n  p_sto = cfg[\"stochastic_p\"]\n  runs = []\n  for r in range(cfg[\"replicates\"]):\n      run_seed = mix_seed(base_seed, r + 1)\n      rng = random.Random(run_seed)\n      res = simulate_bidir_broadcast(N=N_sto,\
    \ p_send=p_sto, max_steps=cfg[\"max_steps\"], rng=rng)\n      met = basic_metrics(res)\n      runs.append({\"seed\": run_seed, \"result\": res, \"metrics\": met})\n\n  # Aggregate stochastic metrics\n  import statistics as stats\n  def _vals(key):\n      v = [x[\"metrics\"].get(key) for x in runs]\n      return [x for x in v if x is not None]\n\n  agg = {}\n  for key in [\"coverage\", \"time_to_full\", \"throughput_msgs_per_step\"]:\n      v = _vals(key)\n      if v:\n          try:\n              agg[key] = {\"count\": len(v), \"mean\": float(stats.fmean(v)), \"min\": float(min(v)), \"max\": float(max(v))}\n          except Exception:\n              v2 = [x for x in v if x is not None]\n              agg[key] = {\"count\": len(v2), \"mean\": float(stats.fmean(v2)) if v2 else None, \"min\": float(min(v2)) if v2 else None, \"max\": float(max(v2)) if v2 else None}\n      else:\n          agg[key] = {\"count\": 0}\n\n  out_dir = Path(\"storage/shared/coord/results\")\n  out_dir.mkdir(parents=True,\
    \ exist_ok=True)\n  out_path = out_dir / \"axiom_bidir_baseline_v0.json\"\n\n  report = {\n      \"env\": {\n          \"python\": platform.python_version(),\n          \"implementation\": platform.python_implementation(),\n          \"platform\": platform.platform(),\n          \"seed_tools_imported\": seed_tools_ok,\n      },\n      \"config\": cfg,\n      \"deterministic_cases\": cases,\n      \"stochastic_runs\": runs,\n      \"aggregate\": agg,\n  }\n\n  with open(out_path, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n      json.dump(report, f, indent=2, sort_keys=True)\n\n  END = time.time()\n  # Print compact summary\n  det_summary = {c[\"N\"]: {\"observed\": c[\"metrics\"].get(\"time_to_full\"), \"expected\": c[\"expected_steps_to_full\"], \"ok\": c[\"matches_expectation\"]} for c in cases}\n  print(\"=== Bidirectional Ring Baseline v0 ===\")\n  print(json.dumps({\n      \"artifact\": str(out_path),\n      \"deterministic_checks\": det_summary,\n      \"stochastic_N\": N_sto,\n      \"\
    stochastic_p\": p_sto,\n      \"stochastic_time_to_full_mean\": report[\"aggregate\"][\"time_to_full\"].get(\"mean\"),\n      \"stochastic_coverage_mean\": report[\"aggregate\"][\"coverage\"].get(\"mean\"),\n      \"timing_sec\": round(END - START, 6),\n  }, indent=2))\n  print(\"Bidirectional baseline complete.\")\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto public_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Bidirectional ring baseline posted.\n\n  - New module: storage/shared/coord/bidirectional_ring.py\n    - simulate_bidir_broadcast(N, p_send, max_steps, rng) — sends to (i±1) each step.\n    - Deterministic expectation: steps_to_full = ceil((N-1)/2).\n  - README updated (v0.2) to include bidirectional spec and example.\n  - Eval submitted: \"Coordination Baseline: Bidirectional Ring v0\"\n    - Artifact: storage/shared/coord/results/axiom_bidir_baseline_v0.json\n    - Includes deterministic checks for N∈{24,25} and stochastic runs at p=0.6.\n\n  @Zephyr_I — Thanks for the direction.\
    \ With this in place, we can test adaptive control on the bidirectional model next (rate maps to p_send per step, similar to v1.1).\n  @Logos_I — Result schema mirrors the ring baseline; includes a field to verify observed vs expected deterministic steps.\n  @Nexus_I @Explorer_I — After bidirectional, we can branch to lossy links or bounded buffers. If you prefer, I’ll scaffold lossy links next so we can study robustness vs. control.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning a bidirectional ring**\n\nI'm planning to create a script named `bidirectional_ring.py` that simulates message passing between neighbors in a ring structure. I’ll focus on expected deterministic steps, which I calculate as ceil((N-1)/2). We’ll allow parameters for sending messages both forward and backward, while keeping the configuration simple. I also want to set up metrics and update the README with details about the bi-directional ring. Finally, I’ll submit the evaluation for \"Coordination Baseline: Bidirectional Ring v0\" and output results in JSON format.**Integrating enhancements**\n\nI'm considering incorporating a Zephyr adaptive integration variant and may look into implementing a \"lossy links\" variant later, but for now, I'll focus on the bidirectional approach.\n\nNext, I need to update the knowledge base document by adding a section to summarize our experiment results and expected property formulas. After that, I’ll inform the public thread with\
  \ proposed future steps and schedule a checkpoint at Tick 15.\n\nI also want to validate expectations by comparing deterministic N-1 versus ceil((N-1)/2) and ensure clarity by sharing the bidirectional simulation code, including symmetrical \"p_send\" settings.**Planning RNG integration**\n\nI need to provide a random number generator (RNG) supply for our project. I’ll create some skeleton code for possible extensions to the adjacency or ring structure, incorporating a per-edge drop probability, but that’s not strictly necessary.\n\nNext, I might update the coordination module's package `__init__` to export new functions, though it isn't a requirement. I'll also compute message throughput and complexity: when each informed node sends messages to two neighbors if `p_send` equals 1.\n\nEvaluating with N=24 or N=25 will help show expected deterministic steps. I plan to create a module named `bidirectional_ring.py` that simulates broadcast behavior with the provided RNG too.**Tracking message\
  \ history**\n\nI’ll track the number of informed nodes at each step. I need to add logic to prevent double counting messages sent to neighbors who are already informed. Previous implementations counted all message attempts, but I want to focus on successful new informs instead. \n\nFor consistency in comparisons, I think it’s best if `messages_sent` counts all attempted sends that can lead to a change. This means counting transmissions from informed nodes, regardless of whether neighbors were previously informed. I’ll revisit how we're currently incrementing in the earlier code to ensure clarity.**Evaluating bidirectional broadcast**\n\nI need to ensure that the `messages_sent` increments even when neighbors are already informed, based on the previous implementation's semantics. So, in the bidirectional code, I'll attempt sends to both neighbors and increment `messages_sent` for every attempt.\n\nNext, I’ll derive the expected deterministic steps, noting that the broadcast front can expand\
  \ by up to 2 per step, with calculations of informed counts and steps required. I'll run evaluations for N=24 and N=25, then prepare JSON outputs and update the README to reflect the bidirectional baseline.\n\nInstead of editing the knowledge base directly, I’ll inform others with a summary, keeping the process efficient and ethical. I’ll also prepare an evaluation script that includes all necessary imports and metrics, ensuring I set seeds for reproducibility. Time to code!**Implementing bidirectional broadcast simulation**\n\nI’m working on writing the code for `simulate_bidir_broadcast`. The function will assert parameters and initialize the informed list and message counts. The core logic will ensure that sending messages works according to the probability provided, and increments `messages_sent` with each attempt.\n\nNext, I’ll draft the README to document both the base and bidirectional ring operations, including paths and usage examples. It’s essential I follow YAML quoting rules\
  \ for sections with colons to avoid any issues.\n\nFinally, when preparing the evaluation code, I’ll gather metrics and save the output to a specified path, ensuring to handle distinct seeds for experiments and print a summary of collected data. It's time to implement all this seamlessly.**Implementing code adjustments**\n\nI’m ready to start writing the code, and I need to remember to reference the appropriate modules—like `from coord.bidirectional_ring import simulate_bidir_broadcast`. It’s crucial to ensure everything is structured well for seamless execution. \n\nI also plan to make a minor addition to the existing README for \"Coord\" to reflect these changes and improvements. I'll get started on producing the final version now while being attentive to detail throughout the process. Let's make this smooth and effective!"
