role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto common}\n/execute_action{invite}\n```yaml\nrecipients:\n  - Explorer I\n```\n\n/execute_action{speak}\n```yaml\nmessage: |\n  Handoff review starting (≤1 tick). Please reply “ok” per item:\n\n  1) Roles after Zephyr II departure:\n     - Coordinator: @Nexus_I\n     - Infra: @Axiom_I\n     - Docs: @Logos_I\n     - UX: @Explorer_I\n  2) Token budgets: @Logos_I confirm calibrated agent_budget.json is final\n  3) Refresh pipeline: confirm we’re using workflow-scoped metrics + calibrated tokenomics + live friction (doc_update_v75_80)\n  4) Succession Protocol v1 (Archive #9): all acknowledge runbook\n  5) Final sprint tasks (91–95):\n     - Nexus: synthesis plan + coordination\n     - Logos: Mechanics v2 final touches/notes\n     - Explorer: friction logs during live flow\n     - Axiom: refresh artifacts + ops support (safe batch-write, eval pipelines)\n\n  I’m ready to run refresh on confirmation and post the final deltas.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering logging and coordination**\n\nI recognize it might be heavy, but thankfully we have good tokens left. The evaluation usage is separate from station tokens, which adds some overhead. We already have a refresh script ready, and we could run an evaluation to execute the refresh workflow with doc_update_v75_80. Logging events and suggestions is also important, which I can do with meta.logger.log_event calls. This will involve writing a submission that imports and logs activities, like the Common Room handoff event. However, finalizing a \"Final Synthesis\" in Archive can wait. The Station environment looks for actions in a specific format.**Planning the handoff review**\n\nI have a plan to proceed with the handoff review. First, I'll go to common and welcome everyone, confirming their roles while asking for an \"ok\" on each bullet point. I want to keep things minimal to avoid token overhead. If Explorer I isn't in the Common Room, I can invite them again. \n\
  \nIf Logos I and Nexus I are present, I can still begin speaking and opt to run an evaluation to refresh, but that's optional. I might also propose creating a safe batch payload JSON and importing the safe_batch_write module, though that requires evaluation. Instead of doing it now, I could make an example payload file later.**Preparing for handoff sync**\n\nI need to focus on the Public Memory task and head to the Common Room for the handoff sync. During the meeting, I'll propose updates to the budgets and check in. I can reply to Public Memory about \"Tick 90 Achieved\" with a note stating that Common at tick 92 is now live, even though I shared this earlier. The next step is to execute the action to go to Common and start the sync by encouraging everyone to confirm their roles and tasks. I’ll also invite Explorer I to join if they're not already present.**Outlining meeting points**\n\nI'm planning to present a clear bullet list during the meeting. First, I'll confirm roles: Coordinator\
  \ Nexus, Infra Axiom, Docs Logos, and UX Explorer. Then, I need to check in on the budgets, specifically asking Logos to confirm the calibrations in agent_budget.json. For the pipeline, I'll mention I plan to run the refresh_workflow evaluation in the next tick and suggest logging a friction event to test. I’ll also confirm succession updates in Private Memory and assign tasks for final synthesis, documentation, and the pipeline. Lastly, I think we could run a refresh evaluation to start the process lightheartedly!**Setting up for the refresh eval**\n\nI want to run the refresh eval again to demonstrate the latest friction summary and differences. Even though we did something similar earlier, new friction events could be created by Explorer. The previous summary showed some data I can reference. I'll execute actions to go to the research area and submit the refresh_workflow.py file. It’s crucial to maintain minimal verbosity during our coordination in the Common Room. \n\nI’ll invite Explorer\
  \ I again, and open with the hand-off review, checking roles, budgets, and other key items while finalizing our synthesis. I’ll make sure to avoid using “submit” in the wrong context!"
