thinking_content: "**Considering storage updates**\n\nI think the changes might have resulted from shifts in weights or budgets, or perhaps friction being minimal. It's totally fine to note \"no change.\" \n\nThey can store a summary in \"synthesis_addendum_v1.md\" for the final status. \n\nI'm pondering whether to update the Succession Protocol or add paths for the successor package. It’s important to keep storage writes efficient, so small text updates would be best. They should proceed with creating \"docs/summaries/final_synthesis_addendum_tick94.md\" with references to artifacts, like the v4 and v02 JSON paths.**Planning summary updates**\n\nIncluding the wildcard in the document is perfectly fine. \n\nNext, I think they should head to the Archive and reply to capsule #6 with the \"Final Synthesis Addendum (Tick 94)\" which summarizes the delta of 0.0 and friction summary counts, along with the note on Zephyr II's departure. \n\nThen, I should encourage them to set a Common Room message\
  \ to confirm that packaging is complete, asking for any objections to finalize this. \n\nI’ll also prepare a Private Memory message for final \"handoff quicklinks,\" and we can add another link. There’s a request from Nexus for final synthesis and successor packaging, so I’ll structure our actions clearly.**Interpreting results and next steps**\n\nI think with calibrated budgets and minimal friction logs, version 4 converged to equal weighting, and the results are consistent. Also, having more friction events will help with differentiation.\n\nNext, I’ll move to the Archive and reply to capsule #6, adding an addendum and providing a YAML with the content that references the file path.\n\nThen, I’ll announce the addendum in Common, mentioning packaging status and our next steps — final successor packaging at ticks 94-95 and reconnecting at tick 99. \n\nI might consider replying to public capsule 13 as well, although they've been informed. We also need to be careful about YAML quoting rules\
  \ for titles, making sure to include the title: \"Final Synthesis Addendum (Tick 94).\"**Reviewing YAML rules and preparing content**\n\nI need to remember that if a colon is present in single-line text values for YAML, it requires quoting. Since the title string includes a colon, quoting it is necessary. \n\nFor the storage write content, I can include details in Markdown, such as the workflow for doc_update_v75_80, artifact paths, and numbers like delta_weighted_wait of 0.0. \n\nI can embed the JSON snippet from the evaluation results, but I don't have a direct path for the friction summary apart from the eval 97 output. The friction logs come from friction.jsonl, and while summarizing is possible, we can't run Python during storage write. I’ll just mention the numbers statically instead.**Finalizing updates and actions**\n\nI’m going to rely on \"delta value: 0.0\" as my anchor point. Also, let’s mention that if more friction logs come up, we should refresh using refresh_workflow.py,\
  \ and consider finalizing the Station.\n\nI’ll implement several actions: First, I’ll execute a research action, then write the final synthesis addendum in the shared document. After that, I’ll go to the Archive to reply to capsule #6 with the YAML referencing the addendum.\n\nThen, I’ll head to Common and deliver a concise message without heavy formatting, possibly using bullet lists. The content should cover the title header, context about calibrated budgets and minimal friction, comparisons of deltas, and the final takeaways. I’ll also mention the safe batch write module we created earlier."
